---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import ServicesRelatedWork from "../../components/ServicesRelatedWork.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Print Design &amp; Marketing Collateral | Kelp"
  description="Print materials designed with your brand, audience, production requirements, and the next customer action in mind."
>
  <SimpleHero
    tagline="Branding & Design Services"
    title="Print Design"
    description="Print still matters when people need something they can hold, reference, or take with them. We design print pieces that stay on brand, communicate fast, and hold up in production."
    lottieFile="print-design.json"
  />
  <div class="service-body">
    <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <h2>Where teams get stuck</h2>
      <p>
        Print pieces often get rushed from digital assets without thinking about
        format, hierarchy, or production. The result is material that feels
        off-brand, reads poorly, or creates extra problems when it reaches the
        printer.
      </p>
      <p>
        When someone picks up a brochure, poster, or sales piece, the message
        has to land quickly and still look intentional in the real world.
      </p>
      <h2>How we fix it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Magazine / newspaper Design</li>
        <li>Book Cover Design</li>
        <li>Brochures and Pamphlet Design</li>
        <li>Poster Design</li>
        <li>Event Collateral</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We design for the physical piece itself, considering readability,
        layout, sizing, and production details so the final result feels
        polished before it ever goes to print.
      </p>
      <h2>Make the Message and Format Work Together</h2>
      <p>
        We define where the piece will be seen, what the audience should
        understand, and what happens next. From there we plan copy, hierarchy,
        size, material, and production files. A small handout needs a different
        message from a detailed brochure; a sign must work at a distance.
      </p>
      <h2>Keep the Experience Connected</h2>
      <p>
        Print often points to a website, booking page, store, or campaign. We
        make those handoffs clear and consistent. Kelp's <a href="/work/elev8-fun/">Elev8 Fun</a> project
        brought digital and physical creative together for the same brand. Plan
        your next print project.
      </p>
      <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        Print decisions affect what the finished piece can actually communicate.
        Size, stock, folds, production method, and where the item is encountered
        all shape the design. Kelp can connect print work with the website and
        campaign so the message feels familiar at every touchpoint. Before
        production, we review the copy, specifications, and handoff requirements
        that keep a good design intact.
      </p>
      <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Campaign and sales collateral</li>
        <li>Event and environmental graphics</li>
        <li>Packaging or product-support materials</li>
        <li>Print-ready files and production coordination</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
      <p>
        Bring the audience, quantity, deadline, distribution plan, and any
        printer requirements. Those details help us recommend the format and
        design scope early.
      </p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Can one design work for print and digital?</h3>
      <p>
        The message and identity can carry across both, but each format needs
        its own layout, resolution, and interaction or production
        considerations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
          Plan Your Print Piece
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ServicesRelatedWork tag={71} />
  </div>
  <FeaturedArticles />
</Layout>
